.TH SPARKY-STEREO-THROW 1 2026-11-25 "sparky-stereo-throw 0.2.0" "Sparky Stereo OS"
.SH NAME
sparky-stereo-throw \- throw the desktop to Google Cast devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparky-stereo-throw list
.br
.B sparky-stereo-throw throw
.I id
.RB [ \-\-wait
.IR seconds ]
.br
.B sparky-stereo-throw stop
.I id
.br
.B sparky-stereo-throw info
.I id
.br
.B sparky-stereo-throw volume
.I id
.IR level | \-
.RB [ mute | unmute ]
.br
.B sparky-stereo-throw status
.I id
.br
.B sparky-stereo-throw choose
.I id
.IR codec | \-
.RI [ mode | \- ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The command of the Throw service (org.sparky.Throw on the session bus). \fBlist\fR prints the Cast devices found on
the network as JSON: identity, name, model, what the model name tells, address, the mode a Throw uses and the state of
its Throw. \fBthrow\fR starts a Throw: the device becomes one more screen of KWin and plays its picture; with
\fB\-\-wait\fR the command waits for the device to play (exit 0) or for the failure (exit 1, the reason printed).
\fBstop\fR ends it. \fBinfo\fR prints what the device says about itself without an account (its setup information and
its receiver's status). \fBvolume\fR sets the device's volume (0 to 1, or \- to leave it) and mute, read back from the
device. \fBstatus\fR prints what a running Throw's device reports (its player state and idle reason) and the bytes it
fetched. \fBchoose\fR keeps a codec (h264, hevc, vp9, vp8, av1) and a mode (\fIW\fRx\fIH\fR@\fIHz\fR) for the device,
\- for the table's choice; a choice the device does not take is refused, and a Throw whose choice this computer cannot
encode is refused with the reason, never made in another codec.
.PP
While a Throw runs, a sound output named "Throw: \fIdevice\fR" exists; what plays into it travels with the picture
(FLAC in Matroska and MP4, Opus in WebM). A speaker or a group gets the sound alone (FLAC in Ogg).
The picture and the sound travel unencrypted between this computer and the device, as the device's own player fetches
them; the stream's server listens on the address facing the device and answers that device only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sparky-stereo-throwrc
[Device][\fIid\fR] Codec= and Mode=\fIW\fRx\fIH\fR@\fIHz\fR: the codec and mode chosen for that device (\fBchoose\fR, or its page).
.TP
.I /usr/share/sparky-stereo-throw/devices.json
The device table: what each model plays.
.SH SEE ALSO
The Throw page in System Settings; the 3D format and eye swap of a network screen are in Display Settings.
